Z||000015||2Low4U||12-20-2001||01:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by banditbalz:<BR><B>that is so cool. Are the seats attached to the interior? I have been looking for a fastback model on ebay for over a year now. Not wiling to pay over $50 US for one. Anyone out there have one they are wiling to part with?<P>You forgot to paint the intake manifold red...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The seats came seperate I had to glue them in, I made the mistake of waiting until they where glued in to paint them, ugh what a pain in the bum.  I painted the intake manifold silver for a different look  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>If it aint low, it aint show.||||reg||1
Z||000016||2Low4U||12-20-2001||01:19 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JetroGT:<BR><B>I've got the same model. I had a problem with the wheels sticking out too far.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Maybe you put the brake's on backwards... I almost put them on so the rotors where sticking out to the front, but they have to go on the other way, if you put them on backwards the tires wouldnt sit properly in the wheel well and will stick out more...<P> Anyways I should be done with the model tonight and will post more pics of it asap.  I need to fix the windshield and one of the sailpanels.  I hate what plastic cement does to clear plastic, makes it all foggy. and I got some glue on the windshield and tried REALLLY fine sand paper to get it off, but just scratched up the windshield a bit, so I will have to get those fixed somehow...<P><BR>------------------<BR>If it aint low, it aint show.<p>[This message has been edited by 2Low4U (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||87ECO5||12-20-2001||02:13 PM||||A guy named Chad Mankins (ChadMan on PFF ... before he left to join the ranks of Miata owners    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">   ) did some nice work on a coupe and IMSA.<P>Check out this thread for photos:<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000002/HTML/20000815-1-001024.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000002/HTML/20000815-1-001024.html</A> ||||reg||14
Z||000018||rogergarrison||12-20-2001||03:24 PM||||wet sand the glass with at least#1500. then some rubbing compound and finish polish it with plain old toothpaste. Almost good as new.||||reg||1
Z||000019||CoolBlue87GT||12-20-2001||03:46 PM||||Another clue about the clear plastic,<P>When you get it all polished, clean it off , wash off well, remove (skin oil) from handling.<BR>Let dry. Make sure no lint is left behind.<P>Then get some Future clear floor wax, pour into clean dish, fill it up enough to "dunk"<BR> the clear plastic in.<P>Dip the clear plastic in, (pull out) then let it sit till it dries. (Important -<BR> let dry fully)<P>While waiting, pour wax back into can, wash out bowl, get a cold one from the fridge, <BR>- enjoy it, maybe then it will be dry enough.<P>When dry, the plastic will shine like crystal.<P>Try this with a test piece, first.<p>[This message has been edited by CoolBlue87GT (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
